  "articleBody": "When you're upgrading light switches for better accessibility, it's easy to fall into some common traps that can actually defeat the purpose. One big mistake is simply placing switches too high, even after you've decided to move them. We know the Fair Housing Accessibility Guidelines recommend switches generally no higher than 48 inches above the floor, and sometimes even lower if there's an obstruction. Another frequent oversight is ignoring how deep an obstruction is. A countertop might be at one height, but its depth can significantly impact how far you can reach over it. And speaking of assumptions, many folks think that a standard residential switch height is automatically accessible for wheelchair users or those with limited reach. It's not always the case! It's crucial to measure and consider the actual reach range of the individuals who will be using the space. Finally, forgetting about coordinating with other accessible controls, like thermostats or outlets, in the same area can lead to a piecemeal solution that's still inconvenient. To avoid these issues, it’s often best to get a professional involved, especially when rewiring or ensuring code compliance. HH Remodeling LLC is here to help you get it right the first time.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
